                        <field name="dc_description">The first major step towards global networking was a thin cable of hardly two centimeters in diameter. In August 1858, the first words between Europe and North America were telegraphed about this - at that time a world sensation: messages that reached their addressee only after almost two weeks ago by ship, now in a matter of seconds they crossed the ocean.<br/>
Today, undersea cables form the backbone of the networked world - only that they are made of glass instead of copper and instead of a sign they can transmit over a trillion units of information per second.<br/>
<br/>
  (Deutsches Technikmuseum) </field>
